INT. CHURCH BASEMENT - GRIEF SUPPORT GROUP - NIGHT

Folding chairs in a circle. Styrofoam cups. A hand-lettered banner reads "WE REMEMBER."

PASTOR LUCILLE MARCHBANKS, 60s, presides over a plate of lemon bars. DWAYNE TOLLIVER, 40s, in a welder's jacket, grips his cup. ILSE BRANDL, 70s, in a lilac cardigan, dabs at her eyes. HAMZA QURESHI, 20s, cross-armed, stares at the floor. BEAU FONTENOT, 30s, jittery, taps a foot. A NEWCOMER, 30s, stands at the door in a rain-soaked coat.

LUCILLE
Everyone, we have a guest.

NEWCOMER
I'll just sit.

DWAYNE
We don't bite.

ILSE
We do, a little.

The newcomer takes the empty chair. Beau offers a lemon bar. The newcomer takes it, turns it over, and sets it on the floor.

LUCILLE
We say our names and our losses. Dwayne, will you begin?

DWAYNE
Dwayne. My brother, Marcus. Eleven months.

ILSE
Ilse. My Walter. Nineteen years.

HAMZA
Hamza. My mother. Last Tuesday.

BEAU
Beau. My dog, Biscuit. And my wife, who left, which is kind of the same.

LUCILLE
Beau.

BEAU
I know. I know.

All eyes turn to the newcomer.

NEWCOMER
Felicia. My daughter. She would have been six.

Silence. The radiator ticks. Ilse reaches across the gap and takes Felicia's hand. Hamza finally looks up.

HAMZA
I'm sorry.

FELICIA
Thank you.

Felicia starts to cry. Dwayne pulls a handkerchief from his jacket and hands it over. The circle closes in a little, chair by chair.

LUCILLE
You're welcome here as long as you need.

FELICIA
Even if I don't talk?

BEAU
Especially then.

ILSE ^
Especially then.

Lucille smiles. She passes the lemon bars around the circle, and one by one, they each take one.
